# Backend rules (FastAPI)

## Layering and allowed imports

    router      -> service, schemas, dependencies, core
    service     -> repository, engines, schemas, integrations, core, events
    repository  -> models, core
    engines     -> stdlib, typing, core.types ONLY
    models      -> core.db only

Forbidden, CI fails on these:

    router      -> repository        (skipping the service layer)
    router      -> models            (leaking ORM to HTTP)
    repository  -> service           (circular)
    engines     -> models, fastapi, sqlalchemy, httpx, redis

## Feature module shape

Every folder under `src/ent/features/<domain>/` has this shape. Omit files the
feature does not need; never add files outside this set without asking.

    router.py          endpoints only
    dependencies.py    feature-specific FastAPI dependencies
    schemas/
      requests.py      input models
      responses.py     output models
      internal.py      DTOs between service and engines
    models.py          SQLAlchemy models owned by this feature
    repository.py      all queries
    service.py         use cases, transaction boundary
    selectors.py       read-optimized queries for dashboards and lists
    policies.py        authorization rules specific to this domain
    events.py          domain events this feature emits
    handlers.py        reactions to events from other features
    constants.py
    exceptions.py
    tests/

## Routers

- Thin. Parse input, call exactly one service method, return a response schema.
- No business rules, no queries, no ORM objects.
- Use the public ULID (`public_id`) in paths, never the integer `id`.
- Declare `response_model` on every endpoint.
- Every state transition is its own sub-resource or verb endpoint
  (`POST /encounters/{id}/sign`), never `PATCH {status: "signed"}`.
- Accept `Idempotency-Key` on POSTs that create clinical records.
- Permissions are declared with the dependency, not checked in the body:

```python
@router.post("/{encounter_id}/sign", response_model=EncounterRead)
async def sign_encounter(
    encounter_id: str,
    user: CurrentUser = Depends(require(Permission.ENCOUNTER_SIGN)),
    service: EncounterService = Depends(get_encounter_service),
) -> EncounterRead:
    return await service.sign(encounter_id, signed_by=user)
```

## Services

- One public method per use case, named for the use case (`create_audiogram`,
  `sign_encounter`), not for the HTTP verb.
- Own the transaction through the unit of work. Emit domain events inside it;
  the bus publishes them only after commit.
- Call engines for anything computed. Never compute clinically meaningful
  values inline.
- Return schemas, never ORM objects.
- Raise domain errors from `core.errors`, never `HTTPException`.
- Receive dependencies through `__init__` so tests can pass fakes.

## Repositories

- Subclass `BaseRepository[Model]` and never bypass `_base_query()`, which
  applies soft-delete and tenant filters.
- Select explicit columns for list endpoints. No `SELECT *`.
- Declare eager loading in the repository method (`selectinload` for
  collections, `joinedload` for single relations). Never rely on lazy loading.
- Every list method paginates. There is no unbounded `list_all`.
- No business rules here. A method that decides something belongs in the
  service.

## Models

- Inherit the mixins rather than repeating columns: `PublicIdMixin`,
  `TimestampMixin`, `AuditMixin`, `SoftDeleteMixin`, `TenantMixin`,
  `VersionMixin`.
- `id` is `BIGINT UNSIGNED` internal; `public_id` is a ULID and is what leaves
  the process.
- Enum-like columns are `VARCHAR` with a `CHECK` constraint, or a lookup table
  when clinicians may extend the list. Never MySQL `ENUM`.
- Money is `DECIMAL(12,3)` plus a currency column. Never float.
- `ON DELETE RESTRICT` on clinical relations. Never cascade.
- JSON columns only for genuinely schemaless payloads. If you will filter,
  sort or aggregate on it, it is a column.

## Schemas

- Inherit `CamelModel` so Python stays snake_case and JSON is camelCase.
- Separate `Create`, `Update`, `Read` models. Never reuse one for input and
  output; that is how write-only fields leak.
- Validate ranges on clinical inputs (frequencies, thresholds, scores, doses).
  A threshold of 500 dB is a typo, not data.
- Reuse the shared value objects from `core.schemas.common`: `CodeableConcept`,
  `Quantity`, `Laterality`, `Period`, `Attachment`, `Provenance`.

## Errors

- Raise `NotFoundError`, `PermissionDeniedError`, `ConflictError`,
  `ValidationError`, `ClinicalSafetyError`, `RecordLockedError`.
- Every error carries a stable namespaced `code` and a `context` dict.
  `prescription.allergy_conflict`, not `"Patient is allergic to penicillin"`.
- Never put a displayable English sentence in an API response.
- A cross-tenant access attempt raises `NotFoundError`, not
  `PermissionDeniedError`.

## Engines (clinical logic)

- Pure functions. Inputs in, dataclass out. No session, no clock, no settings
  lookup. If it needs "now", it is a parameter.
- Each module exposes `VERSION` and `REFERENCE`.
- Return `CalculationResult` carrying value, unit, inputs, formula, version,
  reference and flags.
- Every engine has a test file with cases from the published source, cited in
  the test docstring.
- Never invent a threshold, formula, dose or staging table. Use the documented
  value or ask, and mark uncertainty with `# VERIFY:`.

## Async

- The stack is async end to end. Never call a blocking library inside a request
  handler. CPU-bound or blocking work goes to the worker.
- Do not mix sync and async sessions.

## Testing

- `tests/unit/` for engines, fast, no database.
- `tests/clinical/` for known-answer cases per formula and score.
- `tests/integration/` for repositories and services against real MySQL, each
  test in a rolled-back transaction.
- `tests/api/` for status codes, permissions, tenant isolation, error codes and
  pagination envelope.
- Use the factories in `tests/factories/`. Do not build fixtures by hand.
- Every new endpoint gets a cross-tenant isolation test.
